index f5e5e66..ce82996 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -191,40 +191,69 @@ available.
 
 ## AI Agent Usage
 
-The node is declared `usableAsTool: true` with LLM-friendly descriptions on every parameter. An AI Agent can call it directly from a chat prompt. Example:
+The node is declared `usableAsTool: true`, so it can be attached to an AI Agent.
+Two things about how n8n builds a tool out of a node decide how you have to wire
+it up — get them wrong and the agent will keep answering with the wrong thing
+(most commonly: the list of calendars, whatever you asked for):
+
+1. **Resource and Operation are chosen by you, not by the model.** Both are
+   declared `noDataExpression: true`, which is what n8n requires of these two
+   fields, and it means they can never hold an expression — so `$fromAI()`
+   cannot reach them. **One tool node = exactly one operation.** A tool node
+   left on *Calendar → Get Many* answers every prompt with the calendar list,
+   including "what's on tomorrow" and "create a meeting".
+2. **The tool's input schema is built only from `$fromAI()` placeholders.**
+   n8n scans the node's parameters for `$fromAI(...)` and turns each one into a
+   tool argument. A field filled with a fixed value stays fixed; a field the
+   model should decide has to contain `$fromAI()` explicitly.
+
+### Recommended wiring
+
+Give the agent one CalDAV node per thing it may do, each **renamed** (the node
+name becomes the tool name the model sees) and each with **Description → Set
+manually**, because the auto description collapses to a bare
+`"Get many events in CalDAV"` and drops everything this node documents about its
+fields:
+
+| Node name (tool name) | Resource / Operation | Fields to fill with `$fromAI()` |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `calendar_list`       | Calendar / Get Many | — |
+| `events_in_range`     | Event / Get Many    | Time Min, Time Max |
+| `next_events`         | Event / Get Next    | Lookahead Days |
+| `search_events`       | Event / Search      | Query, Time Min, Time Max |
+| `create_event`        | Event / Create      | Summary, Start, End, additional fields |
+| `update_event`        | Event / Update      | Event URL (or UID), Summary, Start, End |
+| `delete_event`        | Event / Delete      | Event URL (or UID), Occurrence |
+
+Example of a field the model fills, on *Event → Get Many → Time Min*:
 
-> *"Create a calendar event in my primary calendar for tomorrow at 14:00 Berlin
-> time. Title: 'Kickoff with customer'. Duration: 1 hour. Location: 'Zoom — link
-> in the invite'. Invite alice@example.com and bob@example.com. Remind me 1 day
-> and 15 minutes before."*
-
-The agent will populate:
+```
+={{ $fromAI('timeMin', 'Start of the window, ISO 8601 with offset', 'string') }}
+```
 
-- `resource` = `event`, `operation` = `create`
-- `calendar` = picked from the dropdown via `getCalendars`
-- `summary` = `"Kickoff with customer"`
-- `start` = `"2026-04-22T14:00:00+02:00"`, `end` = `"2026-04-22T15:00:00+02:00"`
-- `additionalFields.timezone` = `"Europe/Berlin"`
-- `additionalFields.location` = `"Zoom — link in the invite"`
-- `additionalFields.attendees` = two attendee objects
-- `additionalFields.reminders` = `[{minutesBefore: 1440}, {minutesBefore: 15}]`
+Leave **Calendar** on a fixed pick (or on *🏠 Default Calendar (From
+Credentials)*) unless the agent genuinely has to choose; letting the model
+invent a calendar URL is the most common source of 404s.
 
 ### Recommended system prompt
 
 ```
-You are a calendar assistant with access to a CalDAV tool.
+You are a calendar assistant with access to CalDAV tools.
 
 - Convert any time/date mentioned by the user to ISO 8601 with
-  the Europe/Berlin timezone offset before calling the tool.
+  the Europe/Berlin timezone offset before calling a tool.
   Current time: {{ $now.toISO() }}.
 - Always include "timezone": "Europe/Berlin" in additionalFields.
+- To answer "what is on <date>", call events_in_range — calendar_list
+  only returns calendar names and never contains any event.
+- Read startLocal/endLocal when telling the user when something is;
+  start/end are UTC instants meant for sorting, not for reading out.
 - Before creating: confirm title, start, end in one short sentence.
 - Before deleting: always confirm with the event UID.
 - If the user is vague ("irgendwann"), ask one clarifying question.
-- Use the default calendar (first one returned by getCalendars)
-  unless the user names a specific one.
 ```
 
+
 ## Tested with
 
 | Provider                 | Status               | Notes                                                                                                                                                              |
